<br class="ltx_break">Jena
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Germany 
<br class="ltx_break">paul.gerhardt.rump@uni-jena.de
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mark Blacher
<br class="ltx_break">Institute of Computer Science
<br class="ltx_break">Friedrich-Schiller-University Jena 
<br class="ltx_break">Jena
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Germany 
<br class="ltx_break">mark.blacher@uni-jena.de
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Joachim Giesen
<br class="ltx_break">Institute of Computer Science
<br class="ltx_break">Friedrich-Schiller-University Jena 
<br class="ltx_break">Jena
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Germany 
<br class="ltx_break">joachim.giesen@uni-jena.de
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In 2011, <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> was introduced to NumPy as a practical and convenient notation for tensor expressions in machine learning, quantum circuit simulation, and other fields.
It has since been implemented in additional Python frameworks such as PyTorch and TensorFlow, as well as in other programming languages such as Julia. Despite its practical success, the <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> notation still lacks a solid theoretical basis, and is not unified across the different frameworks, limiting opportunities for formal reasoning and systematic optimization.
In this work, we discuss the terminology of tensor expressions
and provide a formal definition of the <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> language.
Based on this definition, we formalize and prove important equivalence rules for tensor expressions
and highlight their relevance in practical applications.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>
einsum, tensors, tensor expressions, semantic equivalence

</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">Syntax and Semantics of <em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Einsum</em></p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Historically, the most widespread language for describing machine learning operations has been linear algebra.
Linear algebra captures scalars, vectors, and matrices, but does not support operations on higher-order tensors, which are becoming increasingly important in modern machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Moreover, linear algebra assigns each possible operation its own symbol, which becomes impractical when extended to tensors of arbitrary order.
In the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> notation, tensor expressions are written as an operation on index strings, making it a viable option as a machine modeling language.
Through implementations in computational frameworks such as NumPy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and TensorFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> has already proven itself a practical and convenient notation for general tensor expressions.
However, it is not consistently defined across the different frameworks,
which restricts the use of <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> as a common interface and limits opportunities for systematic optimization.
Additionally, the <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> notation lacks a solid theoretical basis,
precluding formal reasoning about and mathematical proofs of its algebraic properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One such property is the fact that semantically equivalent <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions often have many different syntactic representations.
This is highly relevant in practice because the syntactic representation of a tensor expression can strongly impact its suitability for a given task.
Often, one representation may be best suited for efficient evaluation, a second for automatic differentiation, a third for the deduction of relevant properties such as symmetry or convexity,
and yet another for human readability.
For example, the matrix-matrix-vector product <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B\cdot v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B\cdot v</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">einsum<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
which is concise and thus human-readable,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">einsum<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{aligned} ij,j\rightarrow i;A,\texttt{einsum}\left(\begin{aligned} jk,k\rightarrow j;B,v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">einsum</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{aligned} ij,j\rightarrow i;A,\texttt{einsum}\left(\begin{aligned} jk,k\rightarrow j;B,v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which specifies an efficient evaluation order that decomposes the operation into two matrix-vector products, completely avoiding the costly matrix-matrix product <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">einsum<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{aligned} i,ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;1,A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{aligned} i,ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;1,A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
which is suitable for differentiation with respect to the matrix <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the derivative expression
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">einsum<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{aligned} i,jk,k\rightarrow iij;1,B,v\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{aligned} i,jk,k\rightarrow iij;1,B,v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
Without the added ones-vector, all information about the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-axis would be lost.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">So far, however, there are no formal proofs for <span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equivalence rules</span> by which <em id="S1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions can be reshaped.
Therefore, we present a unified definition of the syntax and semantics of <em id="S1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> in this work.
We formally prove the commutativity, associativity, and distributivity of <em id="S1.p2.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>, as well as nesting and denesting rules, and several other semantic equivalence rules.
This addresses the theoretical underpinnings of <em id="S1.p2.2.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> and thus complements existing work on its computational aspects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This article is structured as follows:
Because the tensor terminology that is used in the literature is not unified, we start by introducing the terminology that we use throughout the paper in the next section, <a href="#S2" title="2 Terminology ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></span></a>.
In <a href="#S3" title="3 Syntax ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a>, we propose a syntax for the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> language and explain reasons why we deviate from existing implementations of <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> in certain details.
In <a href="#S4" title="4 Semantics ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span></a>, we define the semantics of <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>,
which are then used in <a href="#S5" title="5 Algebraic properties ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></span></a>, <a href="#S6" title="6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></span></a>, and <a href="#S7" title="7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></span></a>
to prove several properties that are important to efficiently evaluate and automatically differentiate <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions.
The article is then concluded in <a href="#S8" title="8 Conclusions ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Terminology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we clarify the fundamental terminology of tensors and their components.
Formally, we define a tensor as a mapping from positions (multi-indices) to values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Tensors)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\ldots,d_{o}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\ldots,d_{o}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as a set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[d_{1}]\times\ldots\times[d_{o}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[d_{1}]\times\ldots\times[d_{o}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tensor</span> is a mapping <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathcal{X}\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathcal{X}\to R</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positions</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{o})\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{o})\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">entries</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x_{1},\ldots,x_{o})\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x_{1},\ldots,x_{o})\in R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of natural numbers including zero, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of natural numbers without zero, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">order</span> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and say that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">axes</span> with respective <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">axis lengths</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\ldots,d_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\ldots,d_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the literature, an alternative term for axis is ‘mode’.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="99.28" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 99.28" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,99.28) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 76.8 L 614.17 76.8 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 76.8 L 0 99.28 L 614.17 99.28 L 614.17 76.8 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 80.95)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Tensors and data structures</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 11.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="61.58" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Tensors are frequently defined as multidimensional arrays.
In contrast, our more abstract functional definition remains agnostic to any such specific data structure,
and therefore also applies to lists (typically used to represent sparse tensors),
Tensor-Network-Based-Decision-Diagrams (TDDs; useful when representing highly structured tensors) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>,
or any other data structure.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The value set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the definition allows for arbitrary sets <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of possible tensor entries,
the entries in practically relevant problems are typically real numbers (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) or
elements of some other <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">commutative semiring</em>.
In the following, we assume that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative semiring <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\oplus,\otimes)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\oplus,\otimes)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> is an aggregation function and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative combination function.
Beyond the standard arithmetic semiring <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R},+,*)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R},+,*)</annotation></semantics></math>, other notable examples of commutative semirings are the Viterbi and Tropical semirings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scalars.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By convention, we consider the empty product <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{0}[d_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{0}[d_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(~)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(~)\}</annotation></semantics></math> that only contains the empty tuple.
Thus, scalars fit into the above definition as tensors of order <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, a scalar <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in R</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely represented as the tensor <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\{(~)\}\to R,(~)\mapsto c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\{(~)\}\to R,(~)\mapsto c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Delta tensors)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},d_{o}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},d_{o}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a semiring <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, the tensor
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}:[d_{1}]\times\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.}\times[d_{o}]\times[d_{1}]\times\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.}\times[d_{o}]\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}:[d_{1}]\times\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.}\times[d_{o}]\times[d_{1}]\times\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.}\times[d_{o}]\to R</annotation></semantics></math> with entries</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}(p_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},p_{o},q_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},q_{o})=\begin{cases}1,\text{ if }(p_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},p_{o})=(q_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},q_{o})\\
0,\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> otherwise.</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}(p_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},p_{o},q_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},q_{o})=\begin{cases}1,\text{ if }(p_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},p_{o})=(q_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},q_{o})\\
0,\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is called a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">delta tensor</span> of order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2o</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this context, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-element and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-element of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Examples of delta tensors are the scalar <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the unit matrix <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}=\mathds{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}=\mathds{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The precise axis lengths <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},d_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},d_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> of a delta tensor are often omitted when they can be inferred from context.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Syntax</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression has the following generic form:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.0pt;" width="10.0pt"><mtext>. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.0pt;" width="10.0pt"><mtext>. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},T_{n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the hash symbol <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math> replaces the word <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">einsum</span> as a more compact and recognizable marker for the beginning of an <em id="S3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression.
Inside the parentheses, the expression consists of three parts:
A number of index strings <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.0pt;" width="10.0pt"><mtext>. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on the left of the arrow <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>, which we refer to as <span id="S3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">input index strings</span>,
one index string <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on the right of the arrow, which we refer to as the <span id="S3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">output index string</span>,
and a number of subexpressions <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},T_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.0pt;" width="10.0pt"><mtext>. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},\makebox[10.00002pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},T_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right of the output index string, which we refer to as <span id="S3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">arguments</span>.
We also refer to the combination of input index strings, arrow, and output index string as the <span id="S3.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">format string</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A valid <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression must also satisfy the following additional constraints.
For these definitions, let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary number of arguments, and let <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a tensor of order <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">I (Index strings and sets)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Einsum</em> expressions use index strings to specify how one or several input tensors are combined into a single output tensor.
Each index string consists of index symbols <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, where the potentially infinite set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of all index symbols is arbitrary.
We will represent individual index symbols <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> as lowercase letters and index strings <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}\in S^{o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}\in S^{o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> as uppercase letters.
To the left of the arrow <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>, an <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression requires exactly one, possibly empty, index string <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}=(s_{i1},\ldots,s_{io_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}=(s_{i1},\ldots,s_{io_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> for each argument <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On its right is the output index string <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> that corresponds to the result tensor.
The order of index symbols in an index string matters, and multiple appearances of the same index symbol are possible.
We denote the set of index symbols in an index string <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(I_{i})\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(I_{i})\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="115.88" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 115.88" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,115.88) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 90.98 L 614.17 90.98 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 90.98 L 0 115.88 L 614.17 115.88 L 614.17 90.98 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 97.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The index symbol set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in practice</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 8.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:5.48em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="75.76" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 75.76)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Here, we assume a potentially infinite index symbol set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, the choice of an (effectively finite) index symbol set can be very important.
For example, <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> in PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> only allows <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot 26=52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">26</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot 26=52</annotation></semantics></math> alphabetic index symbols.
While literal index symbols are useful for human readability, we recommend that a real-world implementation should additionally accept arbitrary integers in order to handle more axes.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">II (Axis lengths)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In an <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression, each index symbol <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th axis of the tensor <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the expression to be valid, all axes corresponding to the same index symbol must match in length.
In other words, let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the size of the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th axis of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n],j\in[o_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n],j\in[o_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for two identical index symbols <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}=s_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}=s_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> in different locations <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,i^{\prime}\in[n],j\in[o_{i}],j^{\prime}\in[o_{i^{\prime}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,i^{\prime}\in[n],j\in[o_{i}],j^{\prime}\in[o_{i^{\prime}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, it must hold that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}=d_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}}\text{ and thus }[d_{i}]=[d_{i^{\prime}}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and thus </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}=d_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}}\text{ and thus }[d_{i}]=[d_{i^{\prime}}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We denote the uniquely determined length of all axes that an index symbol <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=s_{ij}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=s_{ij}\in S</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}:=d_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}:=d_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">III (Output string)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the result tensor of the <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression to be well-defined, the lengths of its axes must be specified.
This can only be the case if every index symbol in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> also appears in at least one of the input index strings <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the third condition for a valid <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression is:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(I)\subseteq\bigcup_{i\in[n]}\sigma(I_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊆</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(I)\subseteq\bigcup_{i\in[n]}\sigma(I_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="85.1" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 85.1" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,85.1) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 60.19 L 614.17 60.19 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 60.19 L 0 85.1 L 614.17 85.1 L 614.17 60.19 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 66.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Regularity of the language of <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> format strings</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 11.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:3.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 41.86)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For a finite set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of index symbols, the language of <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> format strings over <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is regular.
It follows that the <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> format strings over a finite set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of index symbols can be enumerated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For examples of <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions and corresponding expressions in linear algebra, see <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ III (Output string) ‣ 3 Syntax ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Table <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Examples of the <em id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> notation and the corresponding expressions in linear algebra.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Operation</th>
<th id="S3.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Linear algebra</th>
<th id="S3.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><em id="S3.T1.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">matrix product</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">matrix transposition</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij\rightarrow ji;\;A\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij\rightarrow ji;\;A\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">elementwise product of two vectors</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\otimes y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\otimes y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,i\rightarrow i;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,i\rightarrow i;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">inner product of two vectors (a scalar)</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,i\rightarrow;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,i\rightarrow;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">outer product of two vectors (a matrix)</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,j\rightarrow ij;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,j\rightarrow ij;\;x,y\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">extract the diagonal of a matrix</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.3.7.2.1" class="ltx_text">diag</span>(<math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ii\rightarrow i;\;A\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ii\rightarrow i;\;A\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">broadcast a vector to a diagonal matrix</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.3.8.2.1" class="ltx_text">diag</span>(<math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Differences from popular libraries</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to NumPy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and TensorFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, our definition has two significant differences:
First, the output index string always has to be specified, which is important for <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> to be commutative, and second, the output index string can include the same index symbol more than once, which allows expressions like <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{diag}(v)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{diag}(v)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Semantics</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we define the semantics of the <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> language.
To do so, we first introduce global positions and projections, which allow us to express from which entries in the input
tensors a given entry in the output tensor is computed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Global positions and projections)</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{l}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{l}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all index symbols used in an <em id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the length of the axis that corresponds to the index symbol <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
A global position <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tuple <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{x}_{s_{1}},\dots,\hat{x}_{s_{l}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{x}_{s_{1}},\dots,\hat{x}_{s_{l}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{s}\in[d_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{s}\in[d_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math> for each index symbol <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\prod_{s\in S}[d_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\prod_{s\in S}[d_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all global positions over <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given an index string <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(s^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,s^{\prime}_{o})\in S^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(s^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,s^{\prime}_{o})\in S^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, a global position <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=(\hat{x}_{s_{1}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{s_{l}})\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=(\hat{x}_{s_{1}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{s_{l}})\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> can be projected to a position <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{x}:I):=(\hat{x}_{s^{\prime}_{1}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{s^{\prime}_{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{x}:I):=(\hat{x}_{s^{\prime}_{1}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{s^{\prime}_{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> in a particular tensor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="66.07" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 66.07" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,66.07) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 43.59 L 614.17 43.59 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 43.59 L 0 66.07 L 614.17 66.07 L 614.17 43.59 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 47.74)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:47.32em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="654.78"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:47.32em;">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scalars in <em id="S4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 11.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:1.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="28.37" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 25.25)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scalar operands or results correspond to the empty index string <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with length <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The empty index string projects every possible global position <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the empty tuple, i.e. <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}:\lambda=(~)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.310em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}:\lambda=(~)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The semantics of <em id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>)</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a tensor of order <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with a corresponding index string <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(s_{1},\dots,s_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(s_{1},\dots,s_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> be an output index string that satisfies the conditions in <a href="#S3" title="3 Syntax ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a>,
let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\oplus,\otimes)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\oplus,\otimes)</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative semiring,
and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions.
We define the value of the <em id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">as a sum of products in the semiring <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>-th order tensor with the domain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(T)=[d_{s_{1}}]\times\ldots\times[d_{s_{o}}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(T)=[d_{s_{1}}]\times\ldots\times[d_{s_{o}}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall x\in\text{dom}(T):T(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x\in\text{dom}(T):T(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In words: We calculate the entry at a position <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">aggregating</span> (e.g. summing over) all options <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to assign values to index symbols such that the output index string <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> projects them onto <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each such <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we add the <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">combination</span> (e.g. product) of each respective entry in the individual input tensors.
If an <em id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression consists entirely of scalars, then the set of used index symbols is empty.
The only possible global position in this case is <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=(~)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=(~)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that the aggregation is only over a single term (e.g. a product of scalars in the case of the standard arithmetic semiring).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="101.7" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 101.7" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,101.7) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 76.8 L 614.17 76.8 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 76.8 L 0 101.7 L 614.17 101.7 L 614.17 76.8 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 83.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Different semirings</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 10.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.28em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="61.58" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.15)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">While the language itself remains agnostic of the underlying semiring (i.e. the aggregation and combination operations), an efficient evaluating algorithm typically does not.
For example, when aggregating with the maximum operation, branch-and-bound algorithms might be preferable to algorithms that compute every single entry.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In machine learning, differentiability is an indispensable property because it permits efficient optimization.
But while <em id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions over the standard arithmetic semiring <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R},+,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R},+,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are differentiable, their derivative is not always another <em id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression, but can instead require an elementwise aggregation (here: an elementwise sum) of multiple <em id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions.
As an addition to the <em id="S4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> language, we therefore define elementwise aggregations as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Elementwise aggregation)</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a semiring <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\oplus,\otimes)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\oplus,\otimes)</annotation></semantics></math> and two tensors <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> over that semiring such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(S)=\text{dom}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(S)=\text{dom}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we define the <span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">elementwise aggregate</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\oplus T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\oplus T</annotation></semantics></math> of these tensors as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\hat{x}\in\text{dom}(T):(S\oplus T)(\hat{x})=S(\hat{x})\oplus T(\hat{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\hat{x}\in\text{dom}(T):(S\oplus T)(\hat{x})=S(\hat{x})\oplus T(\hat{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the aggregation operation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> is applied elementwise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="101.01" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 101.01" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,101.01) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 76.1 L 614.17 76.1 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 76.1 L 0 101.01 L 614.17 101.01 L 614.17 76.1 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 82.68)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S4.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Differentiability of <em id="S4.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions over the arithmetic semiring</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 10.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="60.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S4.p6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Expressions that use <em id="S4.p6.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> together with elementwise sums of tensors are closed under differentiation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Such expressions are infinitely differentiable, but may first need to be reshaped into equivalent expressions due to technical reasons.
Specifically, expressions like the one in our introductory example must be reshaped prior to differentiation to avoid the issue of disappearing index information.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Algebraic properties</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the formal definition of <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions presented in the previous sections, we now explore which syntactically different expressions are semantically equivalent.
First, we show three kinds of semantic equivalence rules as algebraic properties of <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>: (1) <em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Einsum</em> is commutative, meaning we can reorder factors, (2) <em id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> is associative, meaning we can change the order of binary operations in which an expression is evaluated, and (3) <em id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> is distributive with respect to the element-wise aggregation <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> of tensors.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Commutativity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Just like in a standard product, commutativity in <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> means that reordering factors does not change the result.
Note, however, that a ‘factor’ in an <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression consists of not only the operand tensor itself, but also its corresponding index string.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Commutativity of <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>)</h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tensors <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with corresponding index strings <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and a result string <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, we can apply any permutation <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> objects without changing the value of the <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression.
That is:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \pi(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})\rightarrow I;\;\pi(T_{1},\ldots,T_{n})\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \pi(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})\rightarrow I;\;\pi(T_{1},\ldots,T_{n})\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 5.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions. Because <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> remains unchanged, both tensors have the same domain.
We show for every position <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in that domain:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\textit{einsum})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">einsum</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\textit{einsum})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{\pi(i)}(\hat{x}:I_{\pi(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{\pi(i)}(\hat{x}:I_{\pi(i)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{commutativity of }\otimes\,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">commutativity of </mtext><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{commutativity of }\otimes\,)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \pi(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})\rightarrow I;\;\pi(T_{1},\ldots,T_{n})\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \pi(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})\rightarrow I;\;\pi(T_{1},\ldots,T_{n})\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\textit{einsum})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">einsum</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\textit{einsum})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In other words, <em id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> is commutative because the combination operation <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="117.68" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 117.68" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,117.68) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 92.78 L 614.17 92.78 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 92.78 L 0 117.68 L 614.17 117.68 L 614.17 92.78 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 99.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Non-commutativity in linear algebra</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 10.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:5.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="77.56" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 75.14)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Matrix-matrix multiplication in linear algebra is not commutative.
That is, in general, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B\neq B\cdot A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B\neq B\cdot A</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is not in contradiction to the commutativity of <em id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>, because the first matrix-matrix product is represented by the <em id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression <math id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the second product is represented by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;B,A\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;B,A\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
These two <em id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions are not generally semantically equivalent, and in fact aggregate over different axes of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Associativity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a matrix multiplication over three matrices <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, associativity means that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(AB)\cdot C=A\cdot(BC)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(AB)\cdot C=A\cdot(BC)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, we can first compute the matrix-matrix product <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B=:D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B=:D</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\cdot C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\cdot C</annotation></semantics></math>, or we can compute <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cdot C=:E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cdot C=:E</annotation></semantics></math> first and then <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot E</annotation></semantics></math>, without changing the result.
For an <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the same concept applies,
meaning we can evaluate an intermediate computation over <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> first and then combine it with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
or we can evaluate an intermediate computation over <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> first and then combine it with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More specifically:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Associativity of <em id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>)</h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given three tensors <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with corresponding index strings <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and result index strings <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I,I_{4},I_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I,I_{4},I_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(I)=\sigma(I_{4})\cup\sigma(I_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(I)=\sigma(I_{4})\cup\sigma(I_{5})</annotation></semantics></math>, the following equalities hold:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{4},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2}\rightarrow I_{4};\;T_{1},T_{2}\end{aligned}\right),T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{4},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2}\rightarrow I_{4};\;T_{1},T_{2}\end{aligned}\right),T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{5}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I_{5};\;T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{5}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I_{5};\;T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof follows immediately from the more fundamental nesting and denesting operations in <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Distributivity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying distributivity to elementwise aggregations of <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions can improve the computational efficiency, as can be seen in the following example from matrix algebra:
Given matrices <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the two equivalent expressions</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB+AC=A(B+C)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB+AC=A(B+C),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the use of distributivity makes computing a second matrix product unnecessary.
Here, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the elementwise addition of matrices, and all products refer to the ordinary matrix-matrix multiplication.
In <em id="S5.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> notation over the standard arithmetic semiring, the expressions read as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle AB+AC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle AB+AC</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)+\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,C\end{aligned}\right)\text{\quad and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right)+\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,C\end{aligned}\right)\text{\quad and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(B+C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(B+C)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,B+C\end{aligned}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,kj\rightarrow ij;\;A,B+C\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we next prove in general that <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> is distributive with regard to the elementwise aggregation operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Distributivity of <em id="S5.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> over aggregations)</h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-th order tensor with index string <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}\in S^{o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}\in S^{o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-th order tensors such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=P\oplus Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=P\oplus Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a result index string <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\oplus T=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\oplus T=U</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right),\text{ and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right),\text{ and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P\oplus Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P\oplus Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 5.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The expressions for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S,T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S,T,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> use the same index symbols and thus have the same set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of global positions.
The shared result string <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(S\oplus T)=\text{dom}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(S\oplus T)=\text{dom}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for every position <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{dom}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{dom}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P\oplus Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;P\oplus Q,T_{2},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}(P+Q)(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}(P+Q)(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(P(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\oplus Q(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(P(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\oplus Q(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{distrib.\ of }\oplus\text{ and }\otimes)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">distrib. of </mtext><mo>⊕</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{distrib.\ of }\oplus\text{ and }\otimes)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(P(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(Q(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(P(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}\left(Q(\hat{x}:I_{1})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=2}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{commutativity of }\oplus)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">commutativity of </mtext><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{commutativity of }\oplus)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=S(x)\oplus T(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=S(x)\oplus T(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Nesting and denesting</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key step when optimizing <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions for efficient evaluation is the computation of a good <em id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contraction path</em>.
A contraction path decomposes a single <em id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression over <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tensors into <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> binary <em id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions, meaning they only have two input tensors each.
For example, the matrix-matrix-vector product <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B\cdot v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B\cdot v</annotation></semantics></math> from the introduction can be written as the following <em id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;\;A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk,k\rightarrow i;\;A,B,v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Associativity allows us to evaluate this expression in two different ways.
One possibility is to compute the matrix-matrix product <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot B</annotation></semantics></math> first, which corresponds to the nested <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,k\rightarrow i;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right),v\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,k\rightarrow i;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,jk\rightarrow ik;\;A,B\end{aligned}\right),v\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The other possibility is to compute the matrix-vector product <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cdot v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cdot v</annotation></semantics></math> first, which results in a different nested <em id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,j\rightarrow i;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} jk,k\rightarrow j;\;B,v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ij,j\rightarrow i;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} jk,k\rightarrow j;\;B,v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Of these two possibilities, the second is computationally more efficient, because it decomposes the operation into two matrix-vector products, completely avoiding the matrix-matrix product.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this example, we used the associativity of matrix products in linear algebra.
Below, we will prove that all <em id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions can similarly be decomposed into a series of multiple smaller expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.p9.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="81.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 81.98" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,81.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 57.08 L 614.17 57.08 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 57.08 L 0 81.98 L 614.17 81.98 L 614.17 57.08 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 63.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.p9.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S6.p9.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Hardness of optimizing contraction paths</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 8.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:3.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="41.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 41.86)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.p9.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S6.p9.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Generally, the choice of contraction path has a significant impact on the computational complexity of the expression.
Optimizing the contraction path is an NP-hard problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and beyond the scope of this work.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Restricted nesting and denesting</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we establish the soundness of the transformations used in contraction paths, which we call <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">restricted nesting and denesting</span>.
This rule is restricted in the sense that there are nested <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions that cannot be denested into one flat expression using only this rule.
Nevertheless, the rule covers all transformations required to specify contraction paths, i.e. to turn a single <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-ary <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression into a hierarchy of nested binary <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Restricted nesting and denesting of <em id="S6.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions)</h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m+n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m+n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-th order tensor with an index string <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of matching length, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u},I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u},I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> also be index strings, and let</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the inner and the outer <em id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression do not have any index symbols in common except for those that appear in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 6.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{U},\mathcal{X}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{U},\mathcal{X}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the global position sets over the index symbol sets <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{U},S_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{U},S_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> for the expressions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:=S_{U}\cup S_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:=S_{U}\cup S_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of index symbols occurring in either expression, and let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\prod_{s\in S}[d_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\prod_{s\in S}[d_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the global position set over these index symbols.
That <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> have no other symbols in common than those in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> can be formally stated as:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Big(\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\sigma(I_{i})\Big)\cap\Big(\bigcup_{j=m+1}^{m+n}\sigma(I_{j})\Big)\subseteq\sigma(I_{u})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\sigma(I_{i})\Big)\cap\Big(\bigcup_{j=m+1}^{m+n}\sigma(I_{j})\Big)\subseteq\sigma(I_{u}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then for all positions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{dom}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{dom}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}U(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}U(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum} for $V$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum} for $V$})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigg(\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}\\
\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{m}T_{j}(\hat{x}_{u}:I_{j})\bigg)\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigg(\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}\\
\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{m}T_{j}(\hat{x}_{u}:I_{j})\bigg)\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum} for $U$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum} for $U$})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}\\
\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}\end{subarray}}\bigg(\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{m}T_{j}(\hat{x}_{u}:I_{j})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}\\
\hat{x}_{v}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}\\
\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}\end{subarray}}\bigg(\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{m}T_{j}(\hat{x}_{u}:I_{j})\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{v}:I_{i})\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{semiring distributivity})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">semiring distributivity</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{semiring distributivity})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I_{v}=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{m+n}T_{i}(\hat{x}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex39.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\ast\text{ see below})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> see below</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ast\text{ see below})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ast)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Observe that for every pair of global positions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{u}\in\mathcal{X}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{v}\in\mathcal{X}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{u}:I_{u}=\hat{x}_{v}:I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a unique <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> that assigns the same value to each index symbol in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{U}\cup S_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{U}\cup S_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, because the shared index symbols <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{U}\cap S_{V}=\sigma(I_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{U}\cap S_{V}=\sigma(I_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> are each assigned the same value by both <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The combinations of all possible pairs <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{u},\hat{x}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{u},\hat{x}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly make up all possible global positions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, both sums are identical.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="103.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 103.43" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,103.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 78.52 L 614.17 78.52 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 78.52 L 0 103.43 L 614.17 103.43 L 614.17 78.52 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 85.1)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Associativity</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 11.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.35em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="63.31" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 60.19)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Since the associativity rules satisfy the condition of <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>,
it follows directly by denesting</span></span>
<span id="S6.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{4},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2}\rightarrow I_{4};\;T_{1},T_{2}\end{aligned}\right),T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\text{\hskip 9.24994ptand\hskip 9.24994pt}\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{5}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I_{5};\;T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">5</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">5</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{4},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2}\rightarrow I_{4};\;T_{1},T_{2}\end{aligned}\right),T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\text{\hskip 9.24994ptand\hskip 9.24994pt}\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{5}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I_{5};\;T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">into
<math id="S6.SS1.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},T_{2},T_{3}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>General nesting and denesting</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Restricted nesting and denesting as defined in <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a> is always sufficient when starting with a completely flat expression.
In practice, however, one may encounter nested expressions that do not conform to the restrictions in <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a> regarding the index symbols in the inner and outer expression.
For example, a matrix-matrix multiplication <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot\text{diag}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>diag</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot\text{diag}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> of a matrix <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and a diagonal matrix <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{diag}(v)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{diag}(v)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed by a nested <em id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression</p>
<table id="S6.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\text{diag}(v)\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\text{diag}(v)\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This expression cannot be denested with the restricted rule, because the output string <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ii" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ii</annotation></semantics></math> of the inner expression does not match the input string <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ik" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ik</annotation></semantics></math> of the outer expression, which is required by <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>. However, a semantically equivalent denested expression</p>
<table id="S6.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,k\rightarrow ik;\;A,v\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik,k\rightarrow ik;\;A,v\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">does exist and is even computationally less expensive, because it avoids the creation of the diagonal matrix and the evaluation of a matrix-matrix product.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In general, the two restrictions of <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a> can lead to two obstructions when denesting.
The first issue is index symbol collisions, which occur where the outer and the inner expression use <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the same</em> index symbol for <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">different</em> purposes.
For example, in the expression</p>
<table id="S6.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk,\rightarrow;\;U,\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk\rightarrow;\;V\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk,\rightarrow;\;U,\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk\rightarrow;\;V\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">the same index symbols <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are used for both of the tensors <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
To retain the same semantics, the denested expression requires a separate set of symbols for each tensor:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk,abc\rightarrow;\;U,V\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ijk,abc\rightarrow;\;U,V\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This problem occurs whenever the outer and the inner expression have an index symbol in common that does not appear in the shared index string (called <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>),
but it can always be resolved by renaming index symbols in either expression until all collisions are eliminated.
Because the scope of a given index symbol is only a single <em id="S6.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression, renaming every occurrence within that expression with a previously unused symbol leaves the result unchanged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second issue is that <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a> assumes that the result index string of the inner expression and the corresponding operand index string of the outer expression match exactly, which may not be the case.
If one or both index strings include duplicate symbols, this cannot be resolved by simply renaming index symbols, because renaming only works when introducing index symbols that are <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not already in use</em> within the expression.
This is, for instance, the case in the example</p>
<table id="S6.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\text{diag}(v)\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\text{diag}(v)\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ik\rightarrow kj;\;A,\#\left(\begin{aligned} i\rightarrow ii;\;v\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">from above.
To resolve this second issue, we replace a direct renaming scheme with the more general concept of a symbol map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Index symbol map)</h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An index symbol map for an index symbol set <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:S\to\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:S\to\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is another index symbol set with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\hat{S}|\leq|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\hat{S}|\leq|S|</annotation></semantics></math>.
The extension of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to entire index strings is denoted as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, an index string <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(s_{1},\ldots,s_{o})\in S^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(s_{1},\ldots,s_{o})\in S^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\ast}(I)=(\nu(s_{1}),\ldots,\nu(s_{o}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\ast}(I)=(\nu(s_{1}),\ldots,\nu(s_{o}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Not every symbol map can be used to denest a nested <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression.
We derive constraints on the symbol map from the inner and outer expression and encode them in an <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">index symbol graph</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Index symbol graph)</h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given nested <em id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right),T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right),T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i},i\in[m+n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i},i\in[m+n]</annotation></semantics></math> are tensors with index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}),\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}),\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are index strings,
the vertex set <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of the undirected index symbol graph is given by</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}\}\cup\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{d}\}\cup\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}\}\cup\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{d}\}\cup\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the edge set <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is derived from the nested expression as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u_{i},u_{j})\in E\&gt;\text{ for all }i,j\in[d]\text{ such that }\&gt;a_{i}=a_{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that </mtext><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u_{i},u_{j})\in E\&gt;\text{ for all }i,j\in[d]\text{ such that }\&gt;a_{i}=a_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(v_{i},v_{j})\in E\&gt;\text{ for all }i,j\in[d]\text{ such that }\&gt;b_{i}=b_{j},\text{ and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that </mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(v_{i},v_{j})\in E\&gt;\text{ for all }i,j\in[d]\text{ such that }\&gt;b_{i}=b_{j},\text{ and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u_{i},x_{i}),\,(v_{i},x_{i})\in E\text{ for all }i\in[d]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u_{i},x_{i}),\,(v_{i},x_{i})\in E\text{ for all }i\in[d].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The vertices in an index symbol graph represent index symbols, and edges connect vertices that must be mapped to the same index symbol.
We get a valid symbol map by mapping every symbol from the original index symbol set <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that is assigned to some vertex in a connected component of the graph to the new symbol in <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for the component.
Note that if the nested expression has index duplications in neither the inner nor the outer expression, the graph has exactly one connected component for every label, and thus the symbol map amounts to a simple renaming of all index symbols.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="86.14" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 86.14" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,86.14) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 61.23 L 614.17 61.23 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 61.23 L 0 86.14 L 614.17 86.14 L 614.17 61.23 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 67.8)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Denesting in practice</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 11.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:3.08em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="46.01" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.55)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In practice, the index symbol graph can be built without the vertices <math id="S6.SS2.p6.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i},i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i},i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symbol map could then explicitly be stated by identifying the graph components.
We have included these vertices because we needed them in the correctness proof of <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 6.5 (General nesting and denesting) ‣ 6.2 General nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></span></a> that is stated below.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(General nesting and denesting)</h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a nested <em id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right),T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right),T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i},i\in[m+n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i},i\in[m+n]</annotation></semantics></math> are tensors with index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}),\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}),\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are index strings.
If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is an index symbol map that respects the restrictions encoded in the index symbol graph constructed from <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \nu^{\ast}(I_{1}),\ldots,\nu^{\ast}(I_{m+n})\rightarrow\nu^{\ast}(I_{v});\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \nu^{\ast}(I_{1}),\ldots,\nu^{\ast}(I_{m+n})\rightarrow\nu^{\ast}(I_{v});\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The theorem suggests a straightforward denesting algorithm in four steps:
(1) construct the index symbol graph,
(2) determine its connected components,
(3) derive the symbol map <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> from the connected components, and
(4) construct the denested expression by using <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> to rename index symbols.
The idea is illustrated on the following example that cannot be denested using only <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vphantom{\overbrace{abbcde}^{\hat{I}_{u}}}\#\left(\begin{aligned} a,b,c,d,e,\smash{\overbrace{abbcde}^{\hat{I}_{u}}}\rightarrow bc;\;v_{1},v_{2},v_{3},v_{4},v_{5},\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,j,k,l\rightarrow\smash{\overbrace{iijkkl}^{I_{u}}};\;v_{6},v_{7},v_{8},v_{9}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vphantom{\overbrace{abbcde}^{\hat{I}_{u}}}\#\left(\begin{aligned} a,b,c,d,e,\smash{\overbrace{abbcde}^{\hat{I}_{u}}}\rightarrow bc;\;v_{1},v_{2},v_{3},v_{4},v_{5},\#\left(\begin{aligned} i,j,k,l\rightarrow\smash{\overbrace{iijkkl}^{I_{u}}};\;v_{6},v_{7},v_{8},v_{9}\end{aligned}\right)\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where the vectors <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}},i\in\{1,\ldots,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}},i\in\{1,\ldots,9\}</annotation></semantics></math> have axis lengths <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that match as required by <em id="S6.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20020v1/graph.svg" id="S6.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:33.927em; height:10.941em;aspect-ratio:451/145;" width="451" height="145"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The index symbol graph for our example that cannot be denested directly by <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The index symbol graph for the example is shown in <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6.2 General nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Figure <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span></a>.
From the connected components of the graph, we derive the new index symbols <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=x,x_{2}=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=x,x_{2}=y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{6}=z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{6}=z</annotation></semantics></math> for the denested expression, and the index symbol map <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> that maps <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, maps <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, and maps <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using this symbol map, we get the denested expression</p>
<table id="S6.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} x,x,y,y,z,x,x,y,z\rightarrow xy;\;v_{1},v_{2},v_{3},v_{4},v_{5},v_{6},v_{7},v_{8},v_{9}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} x,x,y,y,z,x,x,y,z\rightarrow xy;\;v_{1},v_{2},v_{3},v_{4},v_{5},v_{6},v_{7},v_{8},v_{9}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To formally prove the correctness of the denesting algorithm and thus <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 6.5 (General nesting and denesting) ‣ 6.2 General nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></span></a>, we show that the same result expression can be obtained by sequentially applying three rewriting steps, each of which preserves semantic equivalence.
The three rewriting steps are:
(1) introduce delta tensors as additional operands into an <em id="S6.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression to replace any one index symbol with a new index symbol,
(2) apply restricted denesting using <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>,
(3) merge the previously introduced index symbols in accordance with the connected components of the index symbol graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we formally define the splitting and merging operations, prove that these operations keep the transformed expressions semantically equivalent, and finally prove that the three rewriting steps reach the same result as our renaming algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Delta split and delta merge)</h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an <em id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with corresponding index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and operand tensors <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be an index symbol that appears in at least one of the index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> be a new index symbol that does not appear in any of the index strings.
Further, let</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be a second <em id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression in which the index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the index string <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}</annotation></semantics></math> each result from the corresponding index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>
by replacing all, some, or none of the occurrences of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call the reshaping operation that introduces a delta tensor to split the index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> into two separate symbols <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">delta split</span> (or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-split).
The inverse operation, which removes the delta tensor and merges all occurrences of the index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing them with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, is called a <span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">delta merge</span> (or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merge).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="82.68" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 614.17 82.68" width="614.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,82.68) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 60.19 L 614.17 60.19 L 614.17 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#808080;" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 60.19 L 0 82.68 L 614.17 82.68 L 614.17 60.19 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 64.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="553.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.99em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Delta switch</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 10.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.04em;--ltx-fo-height:3.08em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.55)" width="637.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.04em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Because the unit matrix <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}=\mathds{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟙</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}=\mathds{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric, and thus <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}(ab)=\delta_{1}(ba)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}(ab)=\delta_{1}(ba)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>,
a <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-split can equivalently introduce, and a <math id="S6.SS2.p11.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merge remove, the left index symbol of the delta tensor instead of the right.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applying a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-split or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merge
results in a semantically equivalent <em id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 6.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)\text{\hskip 10.22217ptand\hskip 10.22217pt}F=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)\text{\hskip 10.22217ptand\hskip 10.22217pt}F=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}=d_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}=d_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for the lengths of the axes of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit matrix and thus symmetric.
Therefore, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(E)=\text{dom}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(E)=\text{dom}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> still holds even if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}\neq I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}\neq I</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{E},\mathcal{X}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{E},\mathcal{X}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sets of global positions over the respective index symbols <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{E},S_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{E},S_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> for the expressions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show for every position <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{dom}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{dom}(F)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ab,\hat{I}_{1},\ldots,\hat{I}_{n}\rightarrow\hat{I};\;\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\emph{einsum})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\emph{einsum})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a\neq\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a\neq\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\delta_{1}(\hat{x}_{f}:ab)\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\emph{commutativityof}\oplus)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\emph{commutativityof}\oplus)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}1\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a\neq\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}0\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}1\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a\neq\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}0\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\emph{definitionof}\delta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\emph{definitionof}\delta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}\\
\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x\\
\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{neutral element})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">neutral element</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{neutral element})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{e}\in\mathcal{X}_{E}\\
\hat{x}_{e}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{e}:I_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}_{e}\in\mathcal{X}_{E}\\
\hat{x}_{e}:I=x\end{subarray}}\bigotimes\limits_{i=1}^{n}T_{i}(\hat{x}_{e}:I_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex66.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\ast\text{ see below})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> see below</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ast\text{ see below})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}\rightarrow I;\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{n}\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\emph{einsum})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\emph{einsum})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=E(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=E(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ast)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> This equality switches between the index symbol sets <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{F}=S_{E}\cup\{b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{F}=S_{E}\cup\{b\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given the restrictions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x=\hat{x}_{e}:I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{f}:\hat{I}=x=\hat{x}_{e}:I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{f}:a=\hat{x}_{f}:b</annotation></semantics></math>,
each <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{e}\in\mathcal{X}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{e}\in\mathcal{X}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> (a global position in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>) corresponds <em id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">uniquely</em> to an <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{f}\in\mathcal{X}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> (a global position in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>) and vice-versa.
Thus, the sums are equal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we are prepared to prove the correctness of our renaming algorithm, and thus <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 6.5 (General nesting and denesting) ‣ 6.2 General nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></span></a> by showing that our application of the formally verified rewriting rules reaches the same result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 6.9</span></span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall the three rewriting steps: <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-splitting, restricted denesting according to <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merging.
We start with the nested expression</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \hat{I}_{u},I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow I_{u};\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We can assume that the inner and outer expressions have no index symbols in common. Otherwise, we simply replace index symbols in either expression until this condition is satisfied.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(1) <em id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-splitting</em>: Using <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}=(a_{1},\ldots,a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{u}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an index string with new, pairwise distinct index symbols <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-splits in each expression to replace both <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields the intermediary expressions</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} a_{1}x_{1},\ldots,a_{d}x_{d},I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow X;\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} a_{1}x_{1},\ldots,a_{d}x_{d},I_{1},\ldots,I_{m}\rightarrow X;\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,T_{m}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} b_{1}x_{1},\ldots,b_{d}x_{d},X,I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} b_{1}x_{1},\ldots,b_{d}x_{d},X,I_{m+1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},U,T_{m+1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, we only rename exactly one occurrence of the respective index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with each <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-split, meaning that all index strings other than <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> remain unchanged.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(2) <em id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Restricted denesting</em>: Now, the result index string in the inner expression and the corresponding input index string in the outer expression match.
Using <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Restricted nesting and denesting of einsum expressions) ‣ 6.1 Restricted nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></span></a>, we get:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} b_{1}x_{1},\ldots,b_{d}x_{d},a_{1}x_{1},\ldots,a_{d}x_{d},I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} b_{1}x_{1},\ldots,b_{d}x_{d},a_{1}x_{1},\ldots,a_{d}x_{d},I_{1},\ldots,I_{m+n}\rightarrow I_{v};\;\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{1},T_{1},\ldots,\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(3) <em id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merging</em>: The expression has already been denested after the previous step. However, we still want to simplify the expression by getting rid of the delta tensors. Therefore, we iteratively merge the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> delta tensors which have been introduced by <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-splitting.
For each index string <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to a delta tensor, we retain the index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> when merging.
Index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can occur as intermediary results if the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contain duplicate index symbols.
In these cases, we retain the index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\min(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\min(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the proof of <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 6.5 (General nesting and denesting) ‣ 6.2 General nesting and denesting ‣ 6 Nesting and denesting ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Theorem <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></span></a>, it remains to be shown that the expression after the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merging step has the form</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \nu^{\ast}(I_{1}),\ldots,\nu^{\ast}(I_{m+n})\rightarrow\nu^{\ast}(I_{v});\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} \nu^{\ast}(I_{1}),\ldots,\nu^{\ast}(I_{m+n})\rightarrow\nu^{\ast}(I_{v});\;T_{1},\ldots,T_{m+n}\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is an index symbol map that satisfies the conditions encoded in the index symbol graph of the original nested expression, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is the extension of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to index strings.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This is the case because the graph edges that connect symbols in the same position <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math> are represented by the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-tensors with index strings <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
while the graph edges between nodes that contain the same index symbol <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> at different positions <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math> are represented by that symbol appearing in the index strings of multiple <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-tensors,
namely <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sx_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sx_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sx_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sx_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the merging step implicitly computes the connected components of the index symbol graph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Auxiliary simplification rules</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a few additional equivalence rules for <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A semiring <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,\oplus,\otimes)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,\oplus,\otimes)</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-element that is the neutral element of its multiplication <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math>.
Identifying a similar neutral element in an <em id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression is more difficult.
For example, both delta tensors and constant all-ones tensors, i.e., tensors in which the entry at every position is <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, can potentially be ‘neutral’ operands, depending on their index strings.
Reshaping an <em id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression by introducing or removing such neutral operands matters,
for example, when computing the symbolic derivative of an <em id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
During automatic symbolic differentiation, unnecessary delta tensors are systematically created, and all-ones tensors may have to be introduced in order to avoid the issue of disappearing index information, as mentioned in the introduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following proofs demonstrate when and how <em id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expressions involving delta tensors and constant tensors (including all-ones tensors) can be equivalently reshaped.
In particular, we conclude that there is always a way to omit delta tensors, which is not true in other versions of <em id="S7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> such as the one used by NumPy.
Another simple yet important semantic equivalence is the identity operation.
The following semantic equivalence rule can be used as a final simplification step after applying other rules and arriving at the most basic <em id="S7.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">einsum</em> expression <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Identity operation)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tensor of order <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is an index string of length <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> with <em id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">pairwise distinct</em> index symbols, then <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions over the index symbols in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Evidently, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}\left(\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)\right)=\text{dom}(T)=\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}\left(\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)\right)=\text{dom}(T)=\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At every position <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{dom}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{dom}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)=T(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)=T(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if we have duplicate index symbols in the index string <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\neq\text{dom}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mtext>dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\neq\text{dom}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, for <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(i,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(i,i)</annotation></semantics></math> and a corresponding axis length <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[d_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[d_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(T)=[d_{i}]\times[d_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(T)=[d_{i}]\times[d_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a consequence, the aggregation can be empty and thus for <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to have additional <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> entries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Neutral combination)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tensor of order <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I,J</annotation></semantics></math> be index strings such that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(J)\subseteq\sigma(I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(J)\subseteq\sigma(I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} I,J\rightarrow I;\;T,\mathbf{1}_{J}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} I,J\rightarrow I;\;T,\mathbf{1}_{J}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{J}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tensor that takes the scalar value <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> at every position.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I,J\rightarrow I;\;T,\mathbf{1}_{J}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I,J\rightarrow I;\;T,\mathbf{1}_{J}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;T\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions over the index symbols in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Evidently, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(U)=\text{dom}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(U)=\text{dom}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show for every position <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{dom}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{dom}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{J}(\hat{x}:J)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)\otimes 1=V(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>J</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{J}(\hat{x}:J)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}T(\hat{x}:I)\otimes 1=V(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note: If the all-ones tensor introduces new index symbols to the expression (i.e. <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(J)\not\subseteq\sigma(I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(J)\not\subseteq\sigma(I)</annotation></semantics></math>), the same does not hold, because that would add additional global positions to <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Constant vectorization)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a tensor of order <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> with the same entry <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in every position <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})\in\text{dom}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})\in\text{dom}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for an index string <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(i_{1},\dots,i_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(i_{1},\dots,i_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\left(\begin{aligned} ~,i_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},i_{o}\rightarrow I;\;c,\mathbf{1}_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;C\end{aligned}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\left(\begin{aligned} ~,i_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},i_{o}\rightarrow I;\;c,\mathbf{1}_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;C\end{aligned}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the vectors <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are all-ones vectors with lengths corresponding to the axes of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ,i_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},i_{o}\rightarrow I;\;c,\mathbf{1}_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\#\left(\begin{aligned} ,i_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},i_{o}\rightarrow I;\;c,\mathbf{1}_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;C\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow I;\;C\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions over the index symbols in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Evidently, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dom}(U)=\text{dom}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dom}(U)=\text{dom}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
At every position <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})\in\text{dom}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})\in\text{dom}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}c(\hat{x}:())\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{o}\mathbf{1}_{j}(\hat{x}:i_{j})=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}c\otimes 1=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}C(\hat{x}:I)=V(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊗</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>o</mi></munderover><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x)=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}c(\hat{x}:())\otimes\bigotimes\limits_{j=1}^{o}\mathbf{1}_{j}(\hat{x}:i_{j})=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}c\otimes 1=\bigoplus\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:I=x\end{subarray}}C(\hat{x}:I)=V(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Delta substitution)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be an index string of length <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> with pairwise distinct index symbols.
Further, let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> be a delta tensor of order <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}=\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is an all-ones tensor of order <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> with axis lengths matching <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of global positions over the index symbols in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all positions <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{2o})\in\text{dom}(\delta_{o})=\text{dom}\left(\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{2o})\in\text{dom}(\delta_{o})=\text{dom}\left(\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex80"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#\left(\begin{aligned} I\rightarrow II;\;\mathbf{1}_{o}\end{aligned}\right)(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex80.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:II=x\end{subarray}}\mathbf{1}_{o}(\hat{x}:I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>o</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}\hat{x}\in\mathcal{X}\\
\hat{x}:II=x\end{subarray}}\mathbf{1}_{o}(\hat{x}:I)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex80.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">einsum</em></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{\emph{einsum}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.EGx9.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.EGx9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})=(x_{o+1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{2o})\\
0,\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:10.2pt;" width="10.2pt"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">. . .</mtext></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">otherwise</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,(x_{1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{o})=(x_{o+1},\makebox[10.22217pt][c]{.\hfil.\hfil.},x_{2o})\\
0,\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.EGx9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{array}[]{@{}l}(\text{there is exactly one such }\hat{x}.)\\
(\text{there is no such }\hat{x}.)\end{array}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">there is exactly one such </mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">there is no such </mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{array}[]{@{}l}(\text{there is exactly one such }\hat{x}.)\\
(\text{there is no such }\hat{x}.)\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\delta_{o}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\delta_{o}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\text{definition of }\delta_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">definition of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\text{definition of }\delta_{o})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Delta (de)composition)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, it is possible to construct any delta tensor <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> unit matrices <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.10</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Follows directly by combining <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 7.5 (Constant vectorization) ‣ 7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 7.7 (Delta substitution) ‣ 7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Delta removal)</h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any delta tensor or constant tensor can be removed from an <em id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression, leaving only a single scalar (which can be omitted if it is <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>) and exactly one all-ones vector for every index symbol that does not appear elsewhere in the <em id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">einsum</em> expression.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 7.12</span></span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any constant tensor can be reduced to a scalar and ones-vectors by applying <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 7.5 (Constant vectorization) ‣ 7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></span></a> and denesting.
All of the all-ones vectors that do not introduce a new axis can be omitted according to <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 7.3 (Neutral combination) ‣ 7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></span></a>.
Likewise, any delta tensor can be removed with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-merging. To ensure that both index symbols of the <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-tensor appear elsewhere in the expression, we can introduce all-ones vectors with <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 7.3 (Neutral combination) ‣ 7 Auxiliary simplification rules ‣ The Syntax and Semantics of einsum" class="ltx_ref">Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have introduced a unified syntax and semantics for the einsum language and established semantic equivalence rules that provide a solid theoretical foundation for its practical use.
In particular, we proved that einsum expressions over arbitrary commutative semirings are commutative, associative, and distributive with respect to elementwise aggregation.
In addition, we developed general nesting and denesting rules to systematically restructure expressions, along with simplification rules that clarify when delta tensors or constant tensors can be introduced or removed without altering semantics.
Together, these results close the gap between the widespread practical adoption of einsum and its missing theoretical underpinnings,
creating a unified framework that supports principled reasoning about equivalence, differentiation, and optimization of tensor expressions across different computational settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
